Daily checkpoint (3 clicks)

Open GitHub Desktop → it shows your changed files.

Bottom‑left “Summary”: write a message, e.g. checkpoint: <what changed>. Click Commit to main.

Top bar: click Push origin.
Verify: On GitHub.com (clone repo page), the top line over the file list shows your commit message; click it to see details.
Or click “X commits” near main • 1 branch • … to view the history.

Common tasks

Fetch latest from GitHub: Top bar Fetch origin → Pull (if offered).

Undo last local commit (not pushed): Menu Repository → Undo last commit.

Discard a file’s local changes: Right‑click file in Changes → Discard changes.

See/Change the remote URL: Repository → Repository settings → Remote (must end with …Tool-Clone.git).

Rename the repo name in the sidebar: Desktop uses your folder name.

To show “Clone” in the sidebar, rename the folder in Explorer to C:\dev\Alpha-Analytical-Tool-Clone, then in Desktop File → Add local repository… and point to the renamed folder (remove the old entry with Repository → Remove – this does not delete files).

Check the remote without the web: Menu Repository → Open in Command Prompt → run git remote -v (read‑only check).

“No freezes” settings & habits

Do Git in GitHub Desktop, not inside code editors/terminals that may hide auth popups.

Keep the repo outside OneDrive paths; short paths help (e.g., C:\dev\…).

Keep big generated files out of Git via .gitignore (you already have hygiene rules below).

If Git asks to sign in again, Desktop will show a browser auth – complete it once, then pushes work